---
description = "What a watershed document actually is underneath its schema: a root map plus whatever channels its handles point to, each one independently addressed, attached on creation, and resolved through a handle rather than nested by containment — with the limits that layout implies."
layout = "concept-sheet"
concept = "topology"
---

::: g-block
## Your app decides what a document means

*Document* is watershed's generic term for a collection of shared state. Your app gives it a meaning: a saved game, a Kanban board, a project. It doesn't have to look like a page of text, and your users don't have to call it a document.

Separate the *kind* of document from an *instance*. A saved game is a kind; your Friday-night campaign is one instance. The kind describes what the data means and how your app structures it. Each instance has its own identity and state.

- *One kind, one instance.* An app might open the same shared household planner every time.
- *One kind, many instances.* A Kanban app might create a separate document for each board, all using the same schema.
- *Several kinds, many instances.* An app might manage project documents and saved-game documents, each with its own schema and as many instances as users need.

Those kinds are concepts your application defines. Watershed supplies the document model underneath them. A tenant ID and document ID identify an instance; two clients opening the same pair join the same document, not two copies with separate identities.
:::

::: g-block
## The root is one map, not one tree

Every document has exactly one root: a `SharedMap`, reached with `root(document)`, and viewed through your schema with `root_typed`. Its tag comes from the `Document(root)` value itself, fixed at the one place your app first holds it — a `Msg` constructor, usually. Everything your schema declares hangs off that one map.

{data-snippet="foundations-topology-root-typed"}
```gleam
generated
```
:::

::: g-block
## A plain field stores a value; a handle points at a channel

A [`Field`](/foundations/schema) writes its value straight into the map: a string is a string, on the wire and in the map's entries, no indirection. A `ChildField` or a `ChannelField` stores something else entirely — a small JSON object that marks a reference to another channel, elsewhere in the same document. Reading that key back gives you the marker, not the child's contents; you resolve it separately to reach the channel it points to.

{data-snippet="foundations-topology-handle-of"}
```gleam
generated
```
:::

::: g-block
## Detached, attached, resolved

A new channel starts life *detached*: local only, its edits producing no op, invisible to anyone else. It stays that way until you store its handle into a map that's already attached to the document. The runtime then attaches the new channel too, sends its snapshot, and only from that point on does it synchronize with the rest of the world. Create a map and forget to store its handle anywhere, and it just quietly stays a local orphan.

{data-snippet="foundations-topology-create-map"}
```gleam
generated
```

Going the other way, `resolve` turns a handle you read back out of a map into the live channel it names.

{data-snippet="foundations-topology-resolve"}
```gleam
generated
```
:::

::: g-block
## `ChildField` versus `ChannelField`: same handle, different destination

Both store a handle. The difference is only in what kind of thing the handle points to, and the schema layer tracks that at compile time. `ChildField(tag, child)` points at another typed map — a child map with its own schema, its own tag, its own set of fields, within the same document. `ChannelField(tag, kind)` points at a channel that isn't a map at all: a counter, an OR-set, a claims channel, whatever `kind` names. Neither one is a subtree of the parent. Both are peer channels that happen to be reachable from it.
:::

::: g-block
## Independently addressed pieces, not one atomic tree

A watershed document collects data in a set of channels, each with its own address, each syncing on its own terms, tied together only by handles stored as ordinary values. The dice-scoreboard example makes each document a scoreboard. Its roster of players, keyed by id, looks like this on the wire — a root map, a child map, and a further map nested under that:

{data-source-label="examples/scoreboard_cli/src/scoreboard_cli.gleam (diagram)"}
```text
root
├─ "game"      = "watershed dice scores"   (Field(GameRoot, String))
├─ "die_sides" = 6                         (Field(GameRoot, Int))
└─ "players"   = handle ──▶ roster         (ChildField(GameRoot, Roster))

roster
└─ "player-1234" = handle ──▶ player map

player map
├─ "name"
├─ "last_roll"
├─ "total"
└─ "rolls"
```

Nothing about that layout is a single data structure the way a JSON document or an in-memory tree is. Each named piece is its own channel, addressed on its own, with its own merge behavior — [the field atlas](/structures) covers what each kind actually does when two clients touch it at once.
:::

::: g-block
## What that layout costs you

Two honest limits fall straight out of this design, and neither one is a bug to route around:

- *No transaction across channels.* Writing to two channels is two separate operations. There's no way to make both succeed or both fail together — if you need that, keep the related values in one channel instead of splitting them across two.
- *A handle can be briefly unresolved.* Right after a channel is created, its attach operation has to reach every peer before that peer can resolve the handle pointing to it. Until then, resolving returns an error you can retry, not a broken document.


{data-snippet="foundations-topology-resolve-child"}
```gleam
generated
```

:::

::: g-note
*That retry isn't an edge case to special-case away.* Every client that creates a channel and immediately hands its handle to a peer has to expect this. [The next sheet](/foundations/lifecycle) covers the pattern watershed uses to wait it out cleanly at startup, and [runtime · reconnect](/runtime/reconnect) covers what a dropped connection does to an already-resolved handle.
:::

::: g-out
{.annot}
In short

A watershed document is a root map plus whatever channels its handles reach — attached on creation, resolved through the handle rather than nested by containment, with no cross-channel transaction and a short resolve window right after creation. Next: [how a client gets from an empty connection to a document it can build on](/foundations/lifecycle).
:::
